CTF Write-up: Bypassing Chess Game Logic via Client-Side Exploitation
📝 Challenge Information
Name: Crowded Too Early

Category: Web Exploitation

Difficulty: Easy

Objective: Obtain the hidden flag by winning a chess game against the AI.

🔍 Phase 1: Reconnaissance & Analysis
Upon launching the challenge, I was presented with a web-based Chess game. The instructions stated that the flag would be revealed only after defeating the opponent.

Instead of playing the match, I started by analyzing the underlying source code to understand how the flag is handled.

View Page Source: I inspected the HTML source using Ctrl + U.

Identifying JavaScript: I noticed several script tags handling the game engine. One specific script (e.g., app.js or logic.js) appeared to contain the win-condition logic and the flag rendering function.

Code Discovery: While scanning the JavaScript code, I found a variable named flagDisplay (or similar) that was responsible for holding or showing the flag once the "checkmate" condition was met.

🛠️ Phase 2: Exploitation (The Shortcut)
Since the flag logic was executed entirely on the client-side, there was no need to actually win the game. I could interact with the global execution context directly through the browser's developer tools.

Steps taken:
Opened Chrome DevTools (F12).

Navigated to the Console tab.

Targeted the specific variable or object identified earlier.

Executed the following command to bypass the UI and print the flag directly:

JavaScript

console.log(flagDisplay);

Result: The console immediately returned the flag string, bypassing the entire game engine.

🏆 Flag Captured: FLAG{ch3ss_d1dnt_br3@k_y0u_br0k3_th3_l@g1c}

💡 Lessons Learned & Mitigation
The Golden Rule: Never trust the client. Sensitive information (like flags or secrets) should never be stored in client-side JavaScript variables.

Server-Side Validation: The "win" condition should be verified on the server. The flag should only be sent as a response from the backend after a legitimate victory is confirmed.

Obfuscation: While not a complete fix, obfuscating JavaScript makes it harder (though not impossible) for attackers to find sensitive logic quickly.
